<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Edo Castle</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1457">1457</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Edo Castle was a flatland castle in Edo, modern Tokyo, first built by Ōta Dōkan in 1457. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a> made it his seat in 1590, and it was rebuilt on a vast scale as the residence of the Tokugawa <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shoguns</a> from 1603 to 1867, surrounded by moats and the mansions of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daimyo">daimyo</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-restoration">Meiji Restoration</a> it became the imperial residence, and its site is today the Tokyo Imperial Palace.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/edo-castle.jpg" alt="Edo Castle" width="320" height="217" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Edo_P_detail.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
